{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>Walking contrary.</b> Many say it means force and defiance, like a man who strengthens his heart and does not fear, so that he not be defeated; and it has no [parallel] root. Others say it is from the root of קרה (to happen), and the meaning of what is said regarding the Philistines — 'it was chance that His hand touched us' — is the same. According to both interpretations a bet is missing, as in 'for six days,' or 'if thus you walk with me,' 'walking contrary' — many like these. Now, it is written 'if you walk contrary to Me,' and He does not add upon you; and 'your sins' is attached to 'to punish you': the meaning is 'because of your sins.'"], :book :leviticus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra cites 1 Samuel 6:9 (Philistines) and Exodus 16:26 ('six days'); the latter citation appears truncated — 'כי ששת ימים' — likely referring to Exodus 16:26 or a similar verse; left hyper-literal."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:12:45.949749183Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}